{
  "id": "m10-job-sequencing",
  "title": "Deadline Jobs for Profit",
  "source": {
    "name": "Classic job sequencing with deadlines (priority-queue greedy)"
  },
  "rating": 1600,
  "tags": [
    "greedy",
    "heap",
    "scheduling"
  ],
  "statement": "You have `n` jobs, each taking exactly one time unit. Job `i` has a deadline `d_i` and a profit `p_i`. Time slots are `1, 2, 3, ...`; a job scheduled in slot `s` earns its profit only if `s <= d_i`. One job runs per slot and you may skip jobs. Print the maximum total profit.\n\n**Input.** First line `n`. Then `n` lines `d_i p_i`.\n\n**Output.** The maximum profit.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 ≤ n ≤ 1500 in this browser version (the idea scales to 2·10^5)",
    "1 ≤ d_i ≤ 10^9, 1 ≤ p_i ≤ 10^9",
    "The total can exceed 2^31."
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 2000,
  "hints": [
    "A chosen set is feasible iff for every `t`, at most `t` chosen jobs have deadline `<= t`.",
    "Process jobs in deadline order, tentatively accepting each. When more jobs are accepted than the current deadline allows, something must go.",
    "Keep accepted profits in a min-heap. After pushing a job, if the heap size exceeds its deadline, pop the smallest profit."
  ],
  "editorial": "Sort by deadline and keep a min-heap of the profits of accepted jobs. When processing a job with deadline `d`, push its profit; if the heap now holds more than `d` jobs, the set violates the condition `count(deadline <= d) <= d`, so drop the cheapest accepted job. The heap always holds a maximum-profit feasible set among the processed jobs: the feasible sets form a matroid, and dropping the minimum-weight element of the unique violated circuit preserves optimality. Time `O(n log n)`.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "import sys, heapq\nd = sys.stdin.read().split()\nn = int(d[0])\njobs = sorted((int(d[1 + 2 * i]), int(d[2 + 2 * i])) for i in range(n))\n# min-heap of accepted profits\n",
    "javascript": "const n = parseInt(readLine());\n// then n lines 'deadline profit'; implement or reuse a min-heap\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        // PriorityQueue<Long> works as a min-heap\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n; cin >> n;  // read n pairs (deadline, profit); sort + min-heap\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
  "solutions": {
    "python": "import sys, heapq\nd = sys.stdin.read().split()\nn = int(d[0])\njobs = sorted((int(d[1 + 2 * i]), int(d[2 + 2 * i])) for i in range(n))\nheap = []\ntotal = 0\nfor dl, pr in jobs:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, pr)\n    total += pr\n    if len(heap) > dl:\n        total -= heapq.heappop(heap)\nprint(total)\n",
    "javascript": "const lines = [];\nlet line;\nwhile ((line = readLine()) !== null) lines.push(line);\nconst tok = lines.join(' ').split(/\\s+/).filter(s => s.length);\nconst n = Number(tok[0]);\nconst jobs = [];\nfor (let i = 0; i < n; i++) jobs.push([Number(tok[1 + 2 * i]), Number(tok[2 + 2 * i])]);\njobs.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);\n// binary min-heap of profits\nconst h = [];\nfunction push(v) {\n  h.push(v);\n  let i = h.length - 1;\n  while (i > 0) {\n    const p = (i - 1) >> 1;\n    if (h[p] <= h[i]) break;\n    [h[p], h[i]] = [h[i], h[p]];\n    i = p;\n  }\n}\nfunction pop() {\n  const top = h[0];\n  const last = h.pop();\n  if (h.length > 0) {\n    h[0] = last;\n    let i = 0;\n    for (;;) {\n      let l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;\n      if (l < h.length && h[l] < h[m]) m = l;\n      if (r < h.length && h[r] < h[m]) m = r;\n      if (m === i) break;\n      [h[m], h[i]] = [h[i], h[m]];\n      i = m;\n    }\n  }\n  return top;\n}\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const [dl, pr] of jobs) {\n  push(pr);\n  total += pr;\n  if (h.length > dl) total -= pop();\n}\nconsole.log(total);\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        long[][] jobs = new long[n][2];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            jobs[i][0] = sc.nextLong();\n            jobs[i][1] = sc.nextLong();\n        }\n        Arrays.sort(jobs, (a, b) -> Long.compare(a[0], b[0]));\n        PriorityQueue<Long> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();\n        long total = 0;\n        for (long[] j : jobs) {\n            pq.add(j[1]);\n            total += j[1];\n            if (pq.size() > j[0]) total -= pq.poll();\n        }\n        System.out.println(total);\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<pair<long long, long long>> jobs(n);\n    for (auto &j : jobs) cin >> j.first >> j.second;\n    sort(jobs.begin(), jobs.end());\n    priority_queue<long long, vector<long long>, greater<long long>> pq;\n    long long total = 0;\n    for (auto &j : jobs) {\n        pq.push(j.second);\n        total += j.second;\n        if ((long long)pq.size() > j.first) {\n            total -= pq.top();\n            pq.pop();\n        }\n    }\n    cout << total << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
